WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.582
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Upper-Intermediate.

2
00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:09.129
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:16.459
Unit thirty-two: Language and Culture. In this unit, you will discuss languages, culture and ask indirect questions.

4
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.590
lingua franca.

5
00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:23.589
lingua franca means a language used between people with different first languages.

6
00:00:23.889 --> 00:00:27.014
English is the lingua franca of international business.

7
00:00:27.714 --> 00:00:30.390
Your turn. Say: lingua franca.

8
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.463
lingua franca.

9
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.242
dialect.

10
00:00:36.942 --> 00:00:40.161
dialect means a form of a language spoken in one region.

11
00:00:40.461 --> 00:00:43.366
People in Battambang speak a slightly different dialect.

12
00:00:44.066 --> 00:00:46.080
Your turn. Say: dialect.

13
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:49.769
dialect.

14
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:51.552
slang.

15
00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:56.156
slang means very informal words used by particular groups.

16
00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.606
Teenagers use a lot of slang online.

17
00:00:59.306 --> 00:01:01.332
Your turn. Say: slang.

18
00:01:04.032 --> 00:01:04.928
slang.

19
00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:06.662
idiom.

20
00:01:07.362 --> 00:01:10.651
idiom means a phrase whose meaning is different from its words.

21
00:01:10.951 --> 00:01:13.159
'Break a leg' is an idiom meaning 'good luck'.

22
00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:16.001
Your turn. Say: idiom.

23
00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:19.551
idiom.

24
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.528
multilingual.

25
00:01:22.228 --> 00:01:24.936
multilingual means able to speak several languages.

26
00:01:25.236 --> 00:01:27.676
Many Cambodian guides are multilingual.

27
00:01:28.376 --> 00:01:31.064
Your turn. Say: multilingual.

28
00:01:33.764 --> 00:01:34.974
multilingual.

29
00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:37.089
cultural norm.

30
00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:41.298
cultural norm means a behaviour that is normal in a culture.

31
00:01:41.598 --> 00:01:44.375
Taking off your shoes indoors is a cultural norm here.

32
00:01:45.075 --> 00:01:47.762
Your turn. Say: cultural norm.

33
00:01:50.462 --> 00:01:51.394
cultural norm.

34
00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:53.605
translation app.

35
00:01:54.305 --> 00:01:58.058
translation app means a phone app that translates words and speech.

36
00:01:58.358 --> 00:02:01.158
A translation app helped me order food in Japan.

37
00:02:01.858 --> 00:02:04.499
Your turn. Say: translation app.

38
00:02:07.199 --> 00:02:08.676
translation app.

39
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:10.580
code-switching.

40
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.218
code-switching means changing between languages in one conversation.

41
00:02:15.518 --> 00:02:19.364
Code-switching between Khmer and English is common in Phnom Penh.

42
00:02:20.064 --> 00:02:23.030
Your turn. Say: code-switching.

43
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:26.882
code-switching.

44
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:29.883
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:32.473
Do you know why English became a lingua franca?

46
00:02:33.783 --> 00:02:37.388
I'm not sure. Wasn't it because of trade and colonies?

47
00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:42.067
Partly. Can you tell me whether you use slang with your friends?

48
00:02:43.383 --> 00:02:46.883
All the time! Don't you code-switch between languages too?

49
00:02:48.183 --> 00:02:50.963
I do. Haven't you noticed my Khmer improving?

50
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.168
Definitely! Isn't it hard learning idioms in another language?

51
00:02:57.483 --> 00:03:00.795
Grammar focus: Indirect and negative questions.

52
00:03:01.095 --> 00:03:05.068
Indirect questions are polite: Do you know why English is so popular?

53
00:03:05.368 --> 00:03:10.967
Negative questions often expect yes or show surprise: Isn't it difficult to learn idioms?

54
00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:13.951
Use normal word order in indirect questions.

55
00:03:14.251 --> 00:03:18.956
Negative questions often show that you expect the answer 'yes' or that you are surprised.

56
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.916
Now read and listen. One World, Many Voices.

57
00:03:23.216 --> 00:03:26.411
More than seven thousand languages are spoken in the world today.

58
00:03:27.717 --> 00:03:32.654
English has become a lingua franca, but local languages and dialects carry unique culture.

59
00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.935
Many young people are multilingual and use code-switching naturally.

60
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.968
Translation apps help travellers, but they often miss idioms and slang.

61
00:03:44.283 --> 00:03:48.477
Learning a language means learning its cultural norms as well as its words.

62
00:03:49.783 --> 00:03:52.111
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:52.411 --> 00:03:55.977
A language used between people with different first languages is a blank.

64
00:03:56.277 --> 00:03:59.722
A, lingua franca. B, dialect. C, slang.

65
00:04:03.717 --> 00:04:05.612
The answer is A: lingua franca.

66
00:04:07.117 --> 00:04:08.338
Can you tell me where blank?

67
00:04:08.638 --> 00:04:12.803
A, is the station. B, the station is. C, does the station.

68
00:04:16.817 --> 00:04:19.501
The answer is B: the station is.

69
00:04:21.017 --> 00:04:23.785
blank you hungry? You haven't eaten all day!

70
00:04:24.085 --> 00:04:26.461
A, Are. B, Aren't. C, Do.

71
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.043
The answer is B: Aren't.

72
00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:36.734
Changing between languages in one conversation is blank.

73
00:04:37.034 --> 00:04:40.989
A, code-switching. B, idiom. C, cultural norm.

74
00:04:44.983 --> 00:04:46.878
The answer is A: code-switching.

75
00:04:48.383 --> 00:04:51.753
According to the text, what do translation apps often miss?

76
00:04:52.053 --> 00:04:55.637
A, numbers. B, idioms and slang. C, grammar.

77
00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:02.033
The answer is B: idioms and slang.

78
00:05:03.517 --> 00:05:08.052
Now it's your turn to speak. Do you know any idioms in English? Can you explain one?

79
00:05:13.352 --> 00:05:18.536
Isn't 'a piece of cake' a good one? It means something is very easy, like this question!

80
00:05:18.836 --> 00:05:20.255
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:25.400
Great work today! You learned eight new words and indirect and negative questions.

82
00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.746
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

83
00:05:30.046 --> 00:05:33.325
See you in Unit thirty-three. Goodbye!

84
00:05:33.625 --> 00:05:36.196
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

